A||||6||Will||3.4 pushrod & TDC rebuilds - expectations and cost||||1||
Z||000000||Will||09-30-2000||06:37 PM||||I may in the next year be rebuilding a 3.4 pushrod for an '87 GT. I would be using the stock Fiero long runner TPI for its intended purpose: low end grunt. I would also be using ported Fiero iron heads w/ 3-5 angle valve job, roller rockers, etc.<BR>     Is 220 HP and 250 ft-lb (~3500 RPM) from this engine reachable without going to too radical a cam or sacrificing fuel economy or low speed drveability?<P>     What about the 3.4 TDC? How high can this engine go with ported heads, five angle valve job, headers, etc.? Are aftermarket cams available? Is 250-270 and a 7500-8000 redline within reach?<P>How much would each of the above mentioned rebuilds cost?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||10-01-2000||12:30 AM||||To answer your first question about the 3.4 pushrod motor making 220 horsepower I would say no. With the GM performance camshaft, ported heads, 9.7 compression, and ported exhaust manifolds the 3.4 will make about 185 horsepower. With headers like the FOCOA headers it will make 209 horsepower. <P>These predictions were made with Dyno2000.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||08:15 AM||||88formula--I'm not necessarily disagreeing with you, but I've seen a few places that sell rebuild kits for the 2.8s that claim 190hp.  Surely you can get more than that out of the 3.4?  Otherwise, why would anyone spend the extra money for the swap?  If I'm wrong, LMK.  Just seems logical.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sillhouette.com||10-01-2000||10:23 AM||||The 3.4 TDC stock redlines at 7000 rpm.  Your biggest concern is transmission.  If you use the 4T60E you won't be able to go any more than 240HP (I'm still waiting for mine to brake in the GTP).  I personally like the 3.4TDC because of it's high revving lots of power ability.  This ability is not that good at low end.  IF you want low end grunt probably a push rod engine is better.  Going headers larger throttle body basically more flow on the 3.4TDC will hurt it some more on the low end.  Those mods are excellent for high end power.  Not a lot of mods give you such perfomance at 4000 - 7000 rpm.  <P>In closing there are cons and pros to each system.  It depends on what you want.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||10-01-2000||12:53 PM||||Monkeyman, If you are thinking of the rebuild kit that PISA sells were they claim it makes 190 horsepower than that kit is really a 3.1 stroker kit because it includes a stroker crankshaft. I don't know otherwise because those numbers are what I got with the dyno program I have which estimated the stock 2.8 right at 134 horsepower at 4500-RPM, which is the factory rating for 1988.  <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||10-01-2000||05:43 PM||||88, what does the program say a stock 3.4 pushrod will make?  I think all that is different from a 3.1L is the bore (3.662 inches).||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||10-01-2000||06:19 PM||||156 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 200-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm with stock manifolds and mufflers (unported) and 166 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 208 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm with high performance manifolds and mufflers (ported stock logs). I got the stock cam specifications by putting a degree wheel on the stock 3.4 camshaft. I got the stock and ported head flow numbers also from Norris Racing Technologies. They specialize in high performance 3.4 motors for Camaro's and sell ported heads for the 3.4.||||reg||1
